<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Majolus of Cluny</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 910–994</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Saint Majolus, Maieul of Cluny, Mayeul</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Majolus was abbot of Cluny from 954 to 994, under whom the abbey's influence spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, Provence and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and was close to the emperors Otto I and Otto II. In 972 he was captured in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> by Muslim raiders from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fraxinetum">Fraxinetum</a> and held for ransom; the outrage led William of Provence to destroy their base. He is venerated as a saint.</p></div>
